"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT3a, demonstrating extraprostatic extension and positive surgical margins. The seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. No lymph nodes were removed, and nodal sampling was not performed, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. The preoperative PSA was 5.4. The surgical findings, including Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), positive margins, and pT3a stage, are significant prognostic factors. The biopsy grade was consistent with the prostatectomy specimen. The prognosis is influenced by the positive margins and T3a extension. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up information prevents a definitive assessment of the subsequent trajectory. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of regional disease control. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory would be crucial to revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.93 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.76–88.10 months; its coverage has not been validated."
